WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.255
Students usually find distance-time graphs difficult only until the graph starts behaving like a journey story.

2
00:00:06.255 --> 00:00:12.510
The vertical height tells how much distance has been covered by that time.

3
00:00:12.510 --> 00:00:18.765
The slope tells how quickly distance is being added, which means speed.

4
00:00:18.765 --> 00:00:25.020
A flat section means time is passing while the distance stays unchanged, so the object is stopped.

5
00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:31.275
If you read the graph segment by segment, it stops being a line picture and starts becoming a readable motion narrative.

6
00:00:31.275 --> 00:00:37.530
Keep this takeaway in view: Read a distance-time graph one segment at a time and ask what the height and slope mean on that segment.

7
00:00:37.530 --> 00:00:43.785
Height means distance.

8
00:00:43.785 --> 00:00:50.040
The vertical value says how much distance has built up by that moment.

9
00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:56.295
Slope means speed.

10
00:00:56.295 --> 00:01:02.550
Steeper segments mean more distance is added in the same time.

11
00:01:02.550 --> 00:01:08.902
The travel-diary analogy helps a lot here.

12
00:01:08.902 --> 00:01:15.254
Each graph segment is like one diary entry about how distance was changing during that interval.

13
00:01:15.254 --> 00:01:21.607
A steep rising section means the traveller was covering ground quickly.

14
00:01:21.607 --> 00:01:27.959
A shallower rising section means the traveller was still moving, but more slowly.

15
00:01:27.959 --> 00:01:34.311
A flat section means the diary is still recording time, but no extra distance is being added.

16
00:01:34.311 --> 00:01:40.663
When students read every segment as a separate motion entry, the graph starts to speak clearly.

17
00:01:40.663 --> 00:01:47.016
Keep this takeaway in view: The graph becomes readable when you stop asking what the whole line means and start asking what each section means.

18
00:01:47.016 --> 00:01:53.368
Steeper: More distance is added in the same amount of time, so the speed is larger.

19
00:01:53.368 --> 00:01:59.720
Flat: Distance stays constant, so the object is stopped for that interval.

20
00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:05.375
The core formula idea in this lesson is slope equals change in distance divided by change in time.

21
00:02:05.375 --> 00:02:11.030
That is just the speed on that segment.

22
00:02:11.030 --> 00:02:16.685
This works because the vertical axis is distance and the horizontal axis is time.

23
00:02:16.685 --> 00:02:22.340
If the axes were different, the slope would mean something different.

24
00:02:22.340 --> 00:02:27.995
So do not memorize slope in the abstract.

25
00:02:27.995 --> 00:02:33.650
Tie it to the graph type.

26
00:02:33.650 --> 00:02:39.305
On a distance-time graph, slope answers the question, how much distance was added per second?

27
00:02:39.305 --> 00:02:44.960
Keep this takeaway in view: Slope is speed only because the graph is distance against time and you are staying on one segment.

28
00:02:44.960 --> 00:02:50.615
Do use: Two points from the same straight segment that you are being asked about.

29
00:02:50.615 --> 00:02:56.270
Do not use: One point from one segment and one point from a different motion stage.

30
00:02:56.270 --> 00:03:01.925
speed = slope = Δdistance / Δtime.

31
00:03:01.925 --> 00:03:07.580
Choose two points on the same straight segment and compare the changes.

32
00:03:07.580 --> 00:03:13.412
Flat sections deserve their own attention because they are one of the cleanest graph clues in motion.

33
00:03:13.412 --> 00:03:19.244
If the line is horizontal, the distance is not changing as time increases.

34
00:03:19.244 --> 00:03:25.077
That means the object is not moving during that interval.

35
00:03:25.077 --> 00:03:30.909
Students sometimes call this backward motion or zero distance, but neither is correct.

36
00:03:30.909 --> 00:03:36.741
The object may already have travelled some distance earlier.

37
00:03:36.741 --> 00:03:42.573
The flat section simply says no extra distance is being added while the clock keeps running.

38
00:03:42.573 --> 00:03:48.406
Keep this takeaway in view: Flat on a distance-time graph means stopped, not reversed.

39
00:03:48.406 --> 00:03:54.238
Not changing: The distance reading stays at the same height while time moves on.

40
00:03:54.238 --> 00:04:00.070
Meaning: The object is stopped for that interval.

41
00:04:00.070 --> 00:04:04.620
Another rich idea in this lesson is that slope compares rate, not location.

42
00:04:04.620 --> 00:04:09.170
Two straight sections can sit at different heights on the graph and still represent the same speed if they have the same steepness.

43
00:04:09.170 --> 00:04:13.720
That matters because students sometimes focus on where the line is rather than how tilted it is.

44
00:04:13.720 --> 00:04:18.270
The vertical position tells distance.

45
00:04:18.270 --> 00:04:22.820
The tilt tells speed.

46
00:04:22.820 --> 00:04:27.370
Separating those two jobs is what makes graph reading feel mature.

47
00:04:27.370 --> 00:04:31.920
Keep this takeaway in view: Location on the graph and steepness on the graph answer different questions.

48
00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:36.470
Height means distance.

49
00:04:36.470 --> 00:04:41.020
Where the line sits tells how much distance has been covered so far.

50
00:04:41.020 --> 00:04:45.570
Tilt means speed.

51
00:04:45.570 --> 00:04:50.120
How steep the line is tells the rate of motion on that segment.

52
00:04:50.120 --> 00:04:55.158
Take a segment that rises from 6 metres at 2 seconds to 18 metres at 6 seconds.

53
00:04:55.158 --> 00:05:00.196
The distance change is 18 minus 6, which gives 12 metres.

54
00:05:00.196 --> 00:05:05.235
The time change is 6 minus 2, which gives 4 seconds.

55
00:05:05.235 --> 00:05:10.273
Now use slope equals distance change divided by time change.

56
00:05:10.273 --> 00:05:15.311
Twelve divided by four gives 3 metres per second.

57
00:05:15.311 --> 00:05:20.349
Because the segment is straight, that speed is constant on that segment.

58
00:05:20.349 --> 00:05:25.387
Keep this takeaway in view: Use changes from the same segment, not the whole graph unless the question explicitly asks for the whole graph.

59
00:05:25.387 --> 00:05:30.425
Distance change means 12 m.

60
00:05:30.425 --> 00:05:35.464
18 minus 6 gives the distance added on that segment.

61
00:05:35.464 --> 00:05:40.502
Time change means 4 s.

62
00:05:40.502 --> 00:05:45.540
6 minus 2 gives the time covered by that segment.

63
00:05:45.540 --> 00:05:49.072
Ask yourself three quick questions.

64
00:05:49.072 --> 00:05:52.605
What does the graph height at a given time show?

65
00:05:52.605 --> 00:05:56.137
The total distance covered by that time.

66
00:05:56.137 --> 00:05:59.669
What does the slope of one straight segment show?

67
00:05:59.669 --> 00:06:03.202
The speed on that segment.

68
00:06:03.202 --> 00:06:06.734
What does a flat section mean?

69
00:06:06.734 --> 00:06:10.266
The object is stopped for that interval.

70
00:06:10.266 --> 00:06:13.798
Those are the three graph roles this lesson wants you to keep stable.

71
00:06:13.798 --> 00:06:17.331
Keep this takeaway in view: Height, slope, and flatness each have a separate job on a distance-time graph.

72
00:06:17.331 --> 00:06:20.863
Height means distance.

73
00:06:20.863 --> 00:06:24.395
Graph height tells how much distance has built up by that time.

74
00:06:24.395 --> 00:06:27.928
Slope means speed.

75
00:06:27.928 --> 00:06:31.460
Segment steepness tells how quickly distance is being added.

76
00:06:31.460 --> 00:06:36.728
You are ready for the worked example now.

77
00:06:36.728 --> 00:06:41.997
Use the straight segment that goes from 6 metres at 2 seconds to 18 metres at 6 seconds.

78
00:06:41.997 --> 00:06:47.265
Find the distance change first: 18 minus 6 gives 12 metres.

79
00:06:47.265 --> 00:06:52.534
Then find the time change: 6 minus 2 gives 4 seconds.

80
00:06:52.534 --> 00:06:57.802
Use speed equals slope equals distance change divided by time change.

81
00:06:57.802 --> 00:07:03.071
Twelve divided by four gives 3 metres per second.

82
00:07:03.071 --> 00:07:08.339
Because that section is straight, the speed on that segment is constant.

83
00:07:08.339 --> 00:07:13.608
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready: one segment, two changes, one clean division.

84
00:07:13.608 --> 00:07:18.876
Step 1: Pick the two points from the segment being discussed.

85
00:07:18.876 --> 00:07:24.145
Step 2: Calculate the distance change and the time change separately.

86
00:07:24.145 --> 00:07:29.413
Δdistance = 18 - 6 = 12 m.

87
00:07:29.413 --> 00:07:34.682
Δtime = 6 - 2 = 4 s.

88
00:07:34.682 --> 00:07:39.950
Every value comes from the same straight segment of the graph.
